      "problem": "If $a={\\text{i}}^{5},$ then ${a}^{3}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the power law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}={({\\text{i}}^{5})}^{3}={\\text{i}}^{5\\cdot 3}={\\text{i}}^{15}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=15.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n15÷4=3\\,\\text{R}3.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{15}={\\text{i}}^{3}=-\\text{i}.\n$$",
